flow "Three-phase commit (3PC)"
summary "Adds a PreCommit phase to avoid 2PC blocking on timeouts, but loses safety under a network partition."
tags transactions consensus partitions
level advanced
source "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Three-phase_commit_protocol"
related distributed-systems/two-phase-commit

node coord "Coordinator" service tone=primary
group cohort "Participants"
node p1 "Node A" db in=cohort
node p2 "Node B" db in=cohort
edge coord > p1
edge coord > p2
array phase "State" keys=coord,p1,p2 = init init init

scenario commit "Three non-blocking phases"
  step can-commit "Phase 1: CanCommit"
    "The coordinator asks whether participants can execute the transaction. Unlike 2PC, timing out here is always safe to abort."
    state coord active
    send coord > p1 "CanCommit"
    send coord > p2 "CanCommit" with
    state p1,p2 pending
  step vote-yes "Participants vote Yes"
    "Both participants acquire locks, write undo/redo logs, and reply `Yes`. Neither is allowed to commit yet."
    send p1 > coord "Yes"
    send p2 > coord "Yes" with
    label phase#p1 "waiting" with
    label phase#p2 "waiting" with
  step pre-commit "Phase 2: PreCommit"
    "Because everyone voted Yes, the coordinator broadcasts `PreCommit`. This extra round ensures no node can commit while another is still in `init`."
    send coord > p1 "PreCommit"
    send coord > p2 "PreCommit" with
    label phase#coord "pre-commit" with
  step ack-pre "Participants enter pre-committed state"
    "Both nodes acknowledge `PreCommit`. Once a node reaches `pre-commit`, it knows everyone voted Yes and can safely commit on a later timeout."
    detail "Skeen's 1981 non-blocking invariant relies on this buffer state: no participant can commit while another might still be in `init`, and if any surviving node reached `pre-commit`, a recovery coordinator knows every participant voted `Yes`."
    send p1 > coord "ACK"
    send p2 > coord "ACK" with
    state p1,p2 active
    label phase#p1 "pre-commit" with
    label phase#p2 "pre-commit" with
  step do-commit "Phase 3: DoCommit"
    "The coordinator sends `DoCommit`. Both participants finalize the write and release their locks."
    send coord > p1 "DoCommit"
    send coord > p2 "DoCommit" with
    state coord,p1,p2 success
    label phase#coord "committed" with
    label phase#p1 "committed" with
    label phase#p2 "committed" with

scenario partition "Network partition breaks safety" from=commit/vote-yes
  step split "Partition isolates Node B"
    "`PreCommit` reaches Node A, but a network partition drops messages to Node B and cuts Node B off from the coordinator."
    send coord > p1 "PreCommit"
    state p1 active
    label phase#p1 "pre-commit" with
    state coord-p2 error
    callout p2 "Partitioned: never sees PreCommit"
  step diverge "Timeouts choose opposite outcomes"
    "Both nodes time out. Node A is in `pre-commit`, so 3PC tells it to **commit**. Node B is still in `waiting`, so 3PC tells it to **abort**."
    detail "This split-brain is why pure 3PC is rarely used in production: its non-blocking proof assumes bounded delays without partitions. Real distributed SQL stores instead run 2PC over consensus-replicated groups (Paxos or Raft), as in Google Spanner."
    state p1 success
    label phase#p1 "COMMITTED" with
    state p2 error
    effect p2 shake
    label phase#p2 "ABORTED" with
  step inconsistent "Split-brain inconsistency"
    "Node A committed while Node B aborted. Without a quorum intersection like Paxos or Raft, timeouts cannot distinguish a dead peer from a partition."
    focus p1,p2 dim
    callout p1 "Committed"
    callout p2 "Aborted — data diverged" with
